"""Hand-written English method descriptions, part 2: equipment Bodyweight.

Keyed by the v5 catalog slug (slugify of the canonical exercise name).
Merged by build_progression.py as the last description tier; a key that
matches no catalog row fails the build, so typos surface immediately.
"""

DESCRIPTIONS = {
    "archer_pull_ups": "The archer pull up pulls the chest toward one hand while the opposite arm stays nearly straight, shifting almost the entire load onto the working side. Keep the straight arm from bending and pull the chin over the bent-arm hand; it is the bridge between regular pull ups and one-arm work.",
    "archer_push_ups": "The archer push up lowers the chest toward one hand while the other arm slides out straight, loading one side of the chest far harder than a regular push up. Keep the straight arm honest and the hips square; it is the standard progression toward the one-arm push up.",
    "behind_the_neck_pull_up": "The behind-the-neck pull up pulls the bar down behind the head, an old-school variation that demands mobile shoulders and precise control. Only pull to a point behind the head without craning the neck forward; if the shoulders pinch or the head juts, stay with front pull ups.",
    "bench_leg_pull_in": "The bench leg pull-in sits on a bench and pulls the knees in toward the chest and back out, training the abs through a long, controlled range. Grip the bench for stability and keep the lower back from rounding at the extension; slow beats fast.",
    "bicycle_crunch": "The bicycle crunch alternates bringing the opposite elbow toward the knee while the other leg extends, rotating through the trunk continuously. Keep the lower back light on the floor and rotate from the ribs rather than swinging the elbows; moderate speed, full rotation.",
    "bodyweight_calf_raise": "The bodyweight calf raise drives the heels up from the floor through a full ankle extension, training the calves with nothing but bodyweight. Pause at the top and lower through the full stretch; hold a wall for balance but not for assistance.",
    "bodyweight_good_morning": "The bodyweight good morning hinges at the hips with the hands behind the head or at the chest, loading the hamstrings and lower back through a slow, deliberate bow. Keep the spine long and the knees soft; it is a warm-up and hinge-pattern drill rather than a strength lift.",
    "bodyweight_lateral_raise": "The bodyweight lateral raise raises the arms out to the sides with no added load, cueing the side delts in warm-ups and mobility work. Keep the shoulders down away from the ears; think of it as motor-pattern rehearsal for the loaded version.",
    "bodyweight_overhead_press": "The bodyweight overhead press stands and presses the arms overhead with no load, rehearsing the shoulder press pattern in warm-ups and mobility work. Keep the ribs down and reach tall at the top; it is a pattern primer, not a strength builder.",
    "box_squat": "The box squat squats back onto a box or bench, touching it briefly before driving up, which sets the depth and teaches the hip hinge. Sit back fully, keep the shins near vertical and stay braced through the touch; drive up without rocking forward.",
    "clamshells": "The clamshell lies on the side with the heels together and opens the top knee like a shell, training the glute medius through its smallest but most important job. Keep the pelvis perfectly still; if the hip rolls back, the glute has handed off the work.",
    "clap_push_ups": "The clap push-up presses up explosively enough for the hands to leave the floor and clap before the next rep, developing upper-body power. Land with soft elbows and keep the body in one plank throughout; fall back to regular push-ups the moment the landings turn sloppy.",
    "close_grip_pull_ups": "The close-grip pull up hangs with the hands nearly touching and pulls the chest to the bar, shifting the load toward the lower lats and biceps. Keep the shoulders from shrugging and drive the elbows down toward the hips; the narrow grip asks more of the arms.",
    "close_grip_push_ups": "The close-grip push up keeps the hands directly under the shoulders or narrower, shifting the load from the chest toward the triceps. Keep the elbows brushing the ribs on the way down; the body stays in one line exactly as in a regular push up.",
    "cossack_squat": "The Cossack squat sinks sideways onto one loaded leg while the other leg extends out long, training the quads, adductors and ankle mobility at once. Keep the working heel down and the chest up; go only as deep as the hips and ankle allow without collapsing.",
    "crab_dips": "The crab dip lowers the body from a reverse tabletop position by bending the elbows, training the triceps through an unusual shoulder extension angle. Keep the hips high and the elbows tracking straight back; the shoulders should open, not shrug toward the ears.",
    "dead_bug": "The dead bug lies on the back and slowly extends the opposite arm and leg away from the trunk while the lower back stays pressed to the floor. It teaches the core to hold position while the limbs move; move slowly enough that nothing else on the body wobbles.",
    "deficit_push_ups": "The deficit push up places the hands on blocks or dumbbells, letting the chest sink below hand level for a longer range. Keep the body in one line through the deeper bottom; the extra stretch is the growth, and the reason the reps get hard fast.",
    "downward_dog_knee_tuck": "The downward dog knee tuck pulls one knee toward the chest from a downward dog, blending a hamstring stretch with a core crunch. Keep the shoulders stacked over the wrists and round the spine as the knee draws in; alternate sides with control.",
    "downward_dog_to_knee_drive": "The downward dog to knee drive pulls one knee toward the opposite elbow from a downward dog, blending a rotation crunch with a hamstring stretch of the standing leg. Keep the shoulders stacked over the wrists and let the spine round as the knee drives across.",
    "downward_dog_to_plank": "The downward dog to plank flow rocks between the inverted V and the top of a push-up, training shoulder stability and core control in one smooth line. Keep the ribs knitted and the shoulders away from the ears in both positions; move with the breath.",
    "downward_dog_to_upward_dog": "The downward dog to upward dog flow sweeps from the inverted V through a low push-up into the chest-open backbend and back. Keep the shoulders down and the thighs hovering in the upward phase; it trains shoulder strength and spine extension together.",
    "dragon_flag": "The dragon flag lies on a bench or the floor, grips behind the head and lowers the straight body from a vertical position while keeping the hips and legs in one rigid line. It is one of the hardest core exercises there is; tuck the knees first and straighten them over weeks, controlling every degree of the descent.",
    "glute_kickback": "The glute kickback extends one leg straight back and up from a quadruped position, isolating the glute of the working hip. Keep the hips square to the floor, since the leg lifts by squeezing the glute, not by arching the back; small range, hard squeeze.",
    "heel_to_toe_walk": "The heel-to-toe walk walks in a straight line placing the heel of one foot directly before the toes of the other, a balance drill that challenges the ankles and the stabilizing chain of the standing leg. Keep the arms at the sides and the gaze ahead, not down.",
    "hanging_pike": "The hanging pike hangs from a bar and raises the straight legs to hip height or above, piking the hips toward the bar. Keep the swing out of the rep: the hips curl, the legs do not simply swing. It sits between the knee raise and the toes-to-bar.",
    "jackknife_sit_up": "The jackknife sit-up raises the straight arms and legs together from the floor until the hands meet the shins overhead, folding the body in two. Keep the lower back on the floor and hinge at the hips while the ribs curl up; lower both ends slowly.",
    "knee_push_ups": "The knee push up performs the push-up with the knees on the floor, cutting the load roughly in half while keeping the pressing pattern intact. Keep a straight line from the shoulders to the knees with no sagging hips; it is the standard on-ramp to full push ups.",
    "lunge": "The lunge steps forward or backward into a split stance and sinks until both knees approach right angles, training the legs one side at a time. Keep the front knee over the shoe and the torso tall; alternate legs or complete one side first, both are fine.",
    "muscle_ups": "The muscle up pulls from a hang all the way to support above the bar or rings, blending a high pull with a transition and a dip. It demands pulling power, a false grip or a strong kip and patient technique work; progress through high pulls and chest-to-bar reps first.",
    "pause_pull_up": "The pause pull up holds the chin over the bar, or the chest at it, for two to three seconds before lowering, building strength through the top range where most pull ups stall. Keep the shoulders down and the body quiet through the hold; no half-rep at the end.",
    "pike_push_ups": "The pike push up lowers the head toward the floor from a downward-dog position, pressing the body back up with the shoulders overhead. It is the bodyweight bridge toward handstand push ups; keep the hips high and the elbows tracking about 45 degrees back.",
    "pilates_kneeling_side_kick": "The Pilates kneeling side kick kneels tall and sweeps the top leg forward and back in a controlled arc, training the hip stabilizers and the obliques that keep the torso upright. The trunk does not sway; the leg moves and the body stays. Reach long in both directions.",
    "pilates_leg_pull_back": "The Pilates leg pull back holds a reverse plank position and lifts one leg at a time, challenging the posterior chain and the shoulder stabilizers together. Keep the hips level as the leg lifts; the trunk is a plank and the leg is punctuation.",
    "pilates_leg_pull_front": "The Pilates leg pull front holds a high plank and lifts one straight leg slightly off the floor, adding a hip-extension challenge to the anti-rotation plank. Keep the hips square and the shoulders steady; the leg lift is small by design.",
    "pilates_roll_over": "The Pilates roll over lies on the back and rolls the legs overhead until the toes reach beyond the head, articulating the spine one vertebra at a time. Keep the legs straight and the movement slow; it demands control more than flexibility and deserves a warmed-up spine.",
    "pilates_side_bend": "The Pilates side bend holds a side plank on one hand and arcs the top arm overhead, lifting the hips into a long side curve. It trains the obliques and shoulder stabilizers through full range; keep the body in one plane with no twisting toward the floor.",
    "pistol_squat": "The pistol squat descends on a single leg with the other extended straight ahead, the most demanding bodyweight squat there is. Keep the heel down, the shin forward and the hips square; box, heel-elevated and counterweight versions bridge the gap.",
    "pseudo_planche_push_ups": "The pseudo planche push up shifts the hands down beside the hips and leans the shoulders well past the wrists, loading the front delts and straight-arm strength toward the planche. Keep the body hollow and the lean honest; the more forward the shoulders, the harder the rep.",
    "reverse_nordic_curl": "The reverse Nordic curl kneels tall and hinges the whole body backward from the knees, loading the quads through a deep stretch while the hips stay extended. Keep the hips locked forward and move slowly; it is the quad's answer to the Nordic hamstring curl.",
    "reverse_plank_dips": "The reverse plank dip lowers and raises the hips from a reverse plank by bending and straightening the elbows, training the triceps through an unusual extended-shoulder angle. Keep the hips high and the chest open; the shoulders should feel the stretch, not strain.",
    "reverse_tabletop_hip_pulses": "The reverse tabletop hip pulses lift and lower the hips in small pulses from a reverse tabletop, hammering the glutes while the shoulders hold the bridge. Keep the shins vertical and the chest open; squeeze the glutes at the top of every pulse.",
    "ring_face_pull": "The ring face pull leans back holding the rings and pulls the hands apart toward the ears, training the rear delts and rotator cuff against bodyweight. The rings' instability doubles the stabilizer demand; keep the body straight from head to heel and finish with the knuckles beside the ears.",
    "ring_muscle_up": "The ring muscle up pulls from a hang below the rings to a support position above them, the rings adding instability to every phase. The transition demands a deep pull and a fast turn of the elbows; train high pulls and slow transitions before stringing full reps.",
    "ring_push_up": "The ring push up performs a push-up with the hands on unstable rings, forcing the chest, shoulders and core to control the wobble through the whole range. Keep the rings from drifting apart and the body in one line; expect fewer reps than the floor version, and that is the point.",
    "ring_row": "The ring row leans back holding the rings and rows the chest up to the hands, an adjustable bodyweight horizontal pull. Walk the feet forward to make it harder, keep the body rigid like a plank and pull the rings to the ribs; it scales from beginner to brutally hard.",
    "rings_inverted_row": "The rings inverted row hangs beneath the rings with the body straight and rows the chest to the rings, the unstable version of the inverted row. Keep the hips in line with the shoulders and the rings from flaring; the wobble under load is what builds the stabilizers.",
    "scapular_pull_ups": "The scapular pull up hangs from a bar and pulls the shoulder blades down and together without bending the elbows, lifting the body a few inches. It is the foundation drill of all pulling work; keep the arms straight and let the lats do the lifting.",
    "scissor_kicks": "The scissor kick lies on the back and alternates small vertical kicks with the straight legs, holding the low abs under constant tension. Keep the lower back pressed into the floor; if it arches, raise the legs higher. Small, fast, controlled kicks.",
    "single_leg_calf_raise": "The single-leg calf raise drives one heel up at a time, doubling the load on each calf without any equipment. Hold a wall for balance only, keep the knee straight and work through the full stretch at the bottom; the free foot can hook behind the working ankle.",
    "side_lunge": "The side lunge steps wide to one side and sinks the hip back over the bent leg while the other leg straightens, training the legs in the frontal plane. Keep the bent knee over the shoe and the chest up; push the floor away to return rather than flopping up.",
    "side_lying_hip_abduction": "The side-lying hip abduction lifts the top leg from a side-lying position, the classic glute medius builder. Keep the leg in line with the body rather than drifting forward, and the pelvis still; the range is small and the burn is the point.",
    "side_lying_hip_adduction": "The side-lying hip adduction lifts the bottom leg against the floor from a side-lying position, isolating the inner thigh of the down leg. Keep the top knee bent and planted in front; small, controlled lifts with the toe pointed forward.",
    "side_plank_with_leg_lift": "The side plank with leg lift holds a side plank while raising the top leg, stacking a glute medius challenge onto the obliques' isometric hold. Keep the hips high and the body in one plane; if the hips sag, earn the leg lift back with plain side planks first.",
    "split_squat": "The split squat holds a static split stance and sinks the back knee toward the floor, training each leg in place without the step of a lunge. Keep the torso tall and the front heel down; the back knee hovers just above the floor at the bottom.",
    "stability_ball_hip_bridge": "The stability ball hip bridge rests the feet on a stability ball and bridges the hips up, adding an unstable platform to the glute bridge. Keep the ball from drifting and the hips level; the hamstrings and glutes work overtime to steady the ball.",
    "stability_ball_knee_tuck": "The stability ball knee tuck pulls the knees toward the chest from a high plank with the feet on the ball, rolling the ball in with the core. Keep the shoulders stacked over the wrists and the hips from sagging; the ball should move because the abs curled, not because the arms pulled.",
    "stability_ball_push_up": "The stability ball push-up sets the feet or shins on a stability ball during a push-up, adding instability that forces the core to lock the body into one plank. Keep the hands planted and the ball quiet; the wobblier the ball, the better the core works.",
    "stability_ball_push_up_hands_on_ball": "The stability ball push-up with hands on the ball sets the palms on the unstable ball itself, demanding enormous shoulder stability through the press. Keep the elbows tracking back and the ball from sliding; go narrow in range before chasing depth.",
    "stability_ball_wall_squat": "The stability ball wall squat rolls a ball between the back and the wall as the hips sink into a squat, guiding the spine through a smooth, supported arc. Keep the feet far enough forward that the knees stay behind the toes; the ball keeps the back tall and honest.",
    "step_ups": "The step up drives up onto a box or bench from a standing start, training each leg through a full, functional range. Keep the whole foot on the box and push through the heel rather than bouncing off the trailing toe; step down softly under control.",
    "supine_windshield_wipers": "The supine windshield wiper lies on the back with the arms wide and sweeps the straight legs side to side like wipers, rotating through the trunk against gravity. Keep the shoulders pinned to the floor; the legs travel and the chest does not. Bend the knees to make it easier.",
    "straight_bar_dips": "The straight bar dip dips between two parallel bars or on a single straight bar with a pronated grip, a less familiar hand position that loads the chest and triceps afresh. Keep the shoulders down and the lean forward; the unusual grip makes even strong dippers work.",
    "trx_bicep_curl": "The TRX bicep curl leans back holding the straps and curls the body up toward the anchors by flexing the elbows, using bodyweight as the resistance. Keep the body rigid like a plank and the elbows fixed at shoulder height; walk the feet in to add load.",
    "trx_chest_press": "The TRX chest press holds the straps and lowers the body toward the hands in a push-up against the suspension trainer. Keep the straps under tension and the body in one line; the further the feet walk back, the harder the press.",
    "trx_face_pull": "The TRX face pull leans back and pulls the straps apart toward the ears, training the rear delts and rotator cuff with bodyweight. Keep the body straight and the elbows high; the finish has the hands wide and level with the head.",
    "trx_hamstring_curl": "The TRX hamstring curl lies on the back with the heels in the straps and curls the hips up while pulling the heels under the body, a leg curl and bridge in one. Keep the hips high throughout the rep; the slower the pull-in, the more the hamstrings earn.",
    "trx_lunge": "The TRX lunge rests the rear foot in a strap while lunging forward on the front leg, the suspended rear leg adding range and balance demand. Keep the torso tall and the front knee tracking over the shoe; the strap pulls the rear hip into a deeper stretch.",
    "trx_pistol_squat": "The TRX pistol squat uses the straps as a balance aid while descending on one leg, letting the athlete learn the depth before owning the balance. Keep the free leg long and the heel down; lighten the grip gradually until the straps carry nothing.",
    "trx_row": "The TRX row leans back holding the straps and rows the chest up to the hands, the most adjustable horizontal pull in bodyweight training. Keep the body rigid and the straps quiet; walk the feet forward or raise them to scale the load.",
    "trx_squat": "The TRX squat squats while holding the straps, using them for balance and assistance on the way down and up. Keep the grip light, since the legs should do the work the straps merely steady; sit back and down between the heels.",
    "trx_triceps_extension": "The TRX triceps extension faces away from the anchors and extends the elbows to press the body up from a flexed-arm position, a bodyweight skull crusher. Keep the body straight and the head passing between the hands; the closer the feet to the anchors, the harder the lockout.",
    "trx_y_fly": "The TRX Y-fly leans back holding the straps and raises the arms overhead into a Y, training the lower traps and rear delts through a long lever. Keep the body straight and the elbows soft; the shoulder blades do the lifting, not the arms.",
    "v_sit": "The V-sit balances on the sitting bones with the straight legs and torso forming a V, a static core hold that loads the hip flexors and abdominals together. Keep the chest proud and the spine long; if the shoulders round, bring the legs closer to the floor.",
    "v_ups": "The V-up lies flat and lifts the straight legs and torso together until the hands reach the feet, folding the body into a V at the top. Keep the legs and arms long and the movement controlled; the lower back should stay light on the floor on the way down.",
    "wall_push_ups": "The wall push up presses away from a wall while standing, the gentlest push-up progression there is. Keep the body in one straight line from head to heel and the elbows at about 45 degrees; walk the feet further from the wall to add load.",
    "wide_grip_pull_ups": "The wide-grip pull up hangs with the hands well outside the shoulders and pulls the chest to the bar, shifting the load toward the upper lats and teres major. Keep the shoulders from shrugging and the range full; a slightly shorter chin-over-bar path is normal at this width.",
    "wide_grip_push_ups": "The wide-grip push up spreads the hands well outside the shoulders, biasing the outer chest while shortening the triceps' role. Keep the body in one plank and the elbows from flaring too far; the wider the hands, the more careful the shoulder position.",
}
